what Every timing claim this fleet ships is stated in WALL milliseconds read on a pier eight ships share. Row 12's erratum already measured what that costs: a deterministic workload measuring a change of exactly ZERO read a baseline spread of 16.7 to 54.8 percent of its median at load average 8 to 13, so any improvement under roughly a fifth is invisible to a single trial here. The named remedy there was more trials, which is a different method rather than a different unit. This lap tests the other axis: the same workload read in CPU seconds (user plus sys, from the shell's own times builtin, which spawns nothing) beside wall in the same run, so the two units are compared on identical work rather than across sittings. CPU time excludes waiting for a runqueue this fleet keeps full, so the claim is that its relative spread is materially narrower and a saving of a few percent becomes visible where wall needs a fifth. The falsifier is named before the number: if CPU spread sits within a factor of two of wall spread at the same load, the unit buys nothing and the honest finding is that this pier cannot measure small effects at all, which is a harder sentence and belongs on the card either way. tools/fixtures/c/cpu_unit_scan.sh reads both units over N runs of the same deterministic awk spin loop, reports each unit's median, relative spread, and the smallest effect a single trial could distinguish, and reports the observed resolution of the times builtin rather than assuming it. Ratchet nothing, gate nothing: this is a reading about a host. Proven both sides in a pen. Paper in active-designing under the Field setting with horizon, assumptions, falsifier and confidence.
